import definition         stdlib/calculus/continuous-on

theorem identity-function-continuous
  note        f(x) − f(a) is x − a, so a point within ε of a has its value
              within ε of f(a). Take δ = ε.
  ε, δ range over ℝ
  let D ⊆ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let f : D → ℝ                                                       (H2)
  assume for all x ∈ D, f(x) = x                                      (H3)
  then f is continuous on D

1.  For all a ∈ D, for all ε > 0, there exists δ > 0 such that
    |f(x) − f(a)| < ε for all x ∈ D with |x − a| < δ.
    proof
    let a ∈ D                                                         (K1)

    1.1.  For all ε > 0, there exists δ > 0 such that
          |f(x) − f(a)| < ε for all x ∈ D with |x − a| < δ.
          proof
          let ε ∈ ℝ                                                   (K2)
          assume ε > 0                                                (A1)

          1.1.1.  For all x ∈ D with |x − a| < ε, |f(x) − f(a)| < ε.
                  proof
                  let x ∈ D                                           (K3)
                  assume |x − a| < ε                                  (A2)

                  1.1.1.1.  f(x) = x
                            instantiate x := x in H3, from K3

                  1.1.1.2.  f(a) = a
                            instantiate x := a in H3, from K1

                  1.1.1.3.  f(x) − f(a) = x − a
                            calculation
                              f(x) − f(a) = x − f(a)    substitute line 1.1.1.1
                                          = x − a       substitute line 1.1.1.2

                  1.1.1.4.  |f(x) − f(a)| < ε
                            calculation
                              |f(x) − f(a)| = |x − a|    substitute line 1.1.1.3
                                            < ε          A2

          1.1.2.  There exists δ > 0 such that
                  |f(x) − f(a)| < ε for all x ∈ D with |x − a| < δ.
                  exhibit, from A1, 1.1.1

2.  f is continuous on D
    def:continuous-on, from H1, 1
